Apple Continuity dissectors: maintainer notes
=============================================

The files are epan/dissectors/packet-continuity*.c and packet-continuity.h.
The README's "Apple Continuity" section describes what they decode; this file
is for whoever changes them: what each part relies on, how the tests build
their inputs, and how to check the dissectors against real devices.

1. What is supported, and on what evidence
------------------------------------------

  Part                       Status                       Evidence
  -------------------------  ---------------------------  --------------------------------
  BLE advertisements         decoded, received and sent   real PacketLogger traces
  RDLink handshake (M1-M4)   decoded                      real sessions, iPhone and Mac
  RDLink protected frames    decrypted with key material  real sessions; frames the phone
                                                          encrypted included
  Companion envelope         decoded, exchanges paired    real sessions
  OPACK                      decoded, back-references     real sessions; CoreUtils' own
                                                          encoder and decoder
  Media, Camera video        identified by negotiation,   six real streams, 24,004-24,225
                             PT 100 at 24 kHz             ticks per second
  Media, Display video       identified by negotiation,   three real sessions, four tiles
  (AVCVideoStream)           PT 100 at 24 kHz             each, 24,005-24,136 ticks/s
  Media, other streams       identified by negotiation,   none: payload type marked
                             no binding                   opaque, clock rate unknown
  Sidecar capture messages   sub-service and version      real sessions (key 5, key 10)
  Sidecar Display            sessions, control link,      real sessions, iPad on iPadOS
                             streams and media read;      18.3.1, Mac on macOS 15.7.9;
                             objects walked, NOT          touch, Pencil, sidebar, rotation
                             interpreted                  and iPad sleep not exercised

Continuity Camera (and Desk View, which runs inside it), Sidecar's capture
messages and Sidecar Display are separate capabilities. Universal Control,
which macOS sets up with the iPad as soon as a display session ends, is a
fourth: its streams (com.apple.universalcontrol, SYNC/EVNT/CLIP/DRAG) are made
by another process than the display's, so their keys have to be recorded from
that process as well. A Sidecar sub-service
name such as com.apple.sidecar.display is reported when seen, marked as not
interpreted; that is not support for it.

2. Rules the code relies on
---------------------------

Sessions. A connection's session is the handshake's cleartext ephemeral public
key, taken from a PubKey (M1) message only when the message is whole: one OPACK
object walked to its end, exactly one top-level _pd byte string, a TLV8 run that
agrees with its own lengths, exactly one State element of value 1 and exactly one
32-byte public key. Anything else names no session and says why
(continuity.rdlink.handshake_unusable); it never replaces a session already
named. Frames before the naming message are not reported under the session.
Repeated TLV8 elements are refused rather than merged: HomeKit's TLV8 splits
values over 255 bytes into repeated elements, but no element here is that long
and no handshake has been seen to repeat one.

Recognising a connection. The TCP heuristic accepts a first segment that starts
a PubKey frame with an OPACK dictionary whose first key is _pd, or a segment
exactly tiled by frames of known types. Not recognised, by design: a first
handshake segment shorter than 9 bytes, and a handshake whose dictionary puts
another key before _pd and is split over several segments. Such a connection is
picked up at its next exactly-tiled segment, reported without a session. Also
not recognised: a stream sub-connection, which has no handshake, whose first
frame is longer than its first segment. Observed for Universal Control's
streams, whose frames run to several kilobytes and rarely end on a segment
boundary, so such a connection may not be recognised at all.

Media flows. A UDP packet belongs to a negotiated stream when it was captured
after the _streamStart answer, between the two negotiated ports, and each end is
at an address that end announced (_streamAddr) or at its end of the control
connection. Both kinds of address have been seen carrying media. Interfaces are
not compared: on a Mac the control connection and the media use different ones.
No message ending a flow has been seen, so none is assumed: when two
negotiations fit a packet equally the packet is reported as ambiguous. Each
packet's association is fixed on the first pass.

OPACK limits, per message: 16 levels of nesting, 4,096 reference-table entries,
16,384 values, 1 MiB of string and byte-string value (each back-reference
counted at the size of what it names) and 1 MiB of generated member text, each
charged before the work or text it pays for. Observed messages reach at most 439
values, 10,778 bytes of member text and 4,642 bytes of body. On reaching a limit
the walk stops, says which limit, and leaves the rest of the body as undecoded
bytes; the next message is decoded from scratch.

CoreCapture. macOS writes a record's original length as the length of the
payload after the 8-byte stream header, and pads the captured bytes to a multiple
of 4. The frame dissector raises a reported length smaller than the captured one
to the captured one, so the payload length is taken from the frame data.

3. Tests
--------

The tests are test/suite_dissectors/group_continuity.py, run with the rest of
the suite:

    cd build && ninja && ninja test-programs
    python3 -m pytest -c ../pytest.ini --rootdir .. --program-path run \
        ../test/suite_dissectors/group_continuity.py

Every input is built at run time; no capture file is committed and none of the
keys or addresses belong to a real device.

Encrypted fixtures. Protected RDLink frames are sealed at run time by a
ChaCha20-Poly1305 (RFC 8439) written in Python in the test file, independent of
the libgcrypt the dissector uses. The test file checks it against RFC 8439
section 2.8.2 and against frames sealed earlier by Python's `cryptography`. The
construction:

    shared secret   bytes 0x40..0x5f (synthetic)
    ephemeral key   bytes 0xa0..0xbf (synthetic; names the session)
    direction key   HKDF-SHA-512, salt 64 zero bytes, info "ClientEncrypt-main"
                    for frames from the connection's initiator and
                    "ServerEncrypt-main" for the responder; first 32 bytes
    nonce           count of protected frames before this one in this
                    direction, little-endian in 8 bytes, then 4 zero bytes
    associated data the 4-byte frame header: 0x08, then the body length
                    (ciphertext and tag) as 24-bit big-endian
    body            ciphertext of the OPACK plaintext, then the 16-byte tag

The plaintext of each frame is written out in the test with the same OPACK
helpers the cleartext cases use, so what a test asserts is visible beside it.

Sanitizers: configure a separate tree with -DENABLE_ASAN=ON -DENABLE_UBSAN=ON
and CFLAGS "-fsanitize=float-cast-overflow -fno-sanitize-recover=all", build
tshark, editcap and sharkd, and run the same pytest command against it with
UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1.

4. Validating against real devices
----------------------------------

What a device session has to establish, and how to record it. Use your own
devices and keys, and keep captures and key logs out of the repository and out
of public reports: they identify the devices.

Record for every session: device models and exact OS builds (sw_vers; for the
iPhone or iPad, ideviceinfo -k ProductType -k ProductVersion -k BuildVersion),
the connection mode (Wi-Fi, AWDL, cable), the capture points and interfaces, the
duration, the scenario, the capture's SHA-256, the dissector's source revision
(and the SHA-256 of any uncommitted diff), and tshark -v.

Capture on the Mac: tcpdump on awdl0, llw0 and the Wi-Fi interface, from before
the session starts until after it ends; the session's keys from the Rapport
daemon by whatever means you are authorised to use. Then:

    tshark -2 -o continuity.keylog_file:<keys> -r <capture> \
        -Y 'continuity' -T fields -e frame.number -e continuity.session \
        -e continuity.rdlink.read -e continuity.companion.event \
        -e continuity.media.stream_id -e continuity.media.ambiguous \
        -e continuity.media.payload_binding -e continuity.sidecar.service

Continuity Camera (iPhone to Mac): start and stop the camera, use video and the
microphone, switch to Desk View, disconnect and reconnect, and start a later
session that reuses resources. Expect: every control frame read; each _streamStart
answered and its media identified with no ambiguity unless two negotiations
really overlap; video clock 24 kHz (timestamp span over arrival span); the
microphone's payload type and clock as the session shows them -- which, once
observed on a real Mac, can become a binding in packet-continuity-media.c.

Sidecar Display (iPad on iPadOS 18.x to Mac): extended display and mirroring,
disconnect and reconnect, repeated independent sessions, orientation and
resolution changes, sleep and wake, touch, sidebar and Apple Pencil input, and
Camera and Sidecar at once where the devices allow it. Expect: the session's
handshake and control frames read; its sub-service identifiers and transports
named as observed; whatever is decoded to match what the display did. A working
display, a named service or a non-empty tree is not on its own evidence that the
dissection is right. Until such a session has been examined, Display support is
not claimed.
